    Add a single-map summand to the general sumcheck
    
    The general masked sumcheck gains a one-map summand of degree one and an inner sumcheck over a dense Boolean cube: every variable is summed plainly, so a round polynomial is linear and its two coefficients are the round's sums at the variable's two points. A focused test proves a map's cube sum, verifies it, and shows that a changed round leaves another output claim that the caller binds to committed messages.
    
    This is partial progress: the merge that batches weight claims uses this summand, and its transcript wiring is the next step.

